  "decision_conditions": [
    "The proposed change of use of the outbuilding from an ancillary residential garage to a nail salon introduces an independent commercial use within a residential curtilage, in a location outside designated town, district and local centres. The development represents an inappropriate form of development which conflicts with the established residential character and function of the area and fails to accord with the planned approach to the location of service and commercial uses. The proposal is contrary to Chapter 7 of the NPPF (2024), Policy E9 of the London Plan (2021), and Policies DMD 25, DMD 26 and DMD 29 of the Enfield Development Management Document (2014).",
    "The replacement of the original garage doors with a shopfront results in a form of development that is visually and functionally at odds with the prevailing character of Pymmes Road, which is characterised by modest ancillary garage structures serving nearby residential properties. The introduction of a commercial shopfront in this location fails to respect the established townscape and erodes the uniform and unobtrusive appearance of the garage row. The proposal therefore represents poor design and fails to respond positively to its context, contrary to Chapter 12 of the NPPF (2024), Policy D3 of the London Plan (2021), and Policy DMD 37 of the Enfield Development Management Document (2014).",
    "The use of the outbuilding as a nail salon introduces independent commercial activity into a rear residential curtilage. While the scale of the use is modest, the change of use is incompatible with the predominantly residential character of the area and has the potential to alter the quiet and private nature of the locality. The proposal is therefore contrary to the Chapter 12 NPPF (2024), Policy D3 of the London Plan (2021), and Policy DMD 37 of the Enfield Development Management Document (2014).",
    "This proposed would exacerbate existing parking pressures and congestion and could give rise to obstruction or conflict between vehicles and pedestrians, creating a potential risk to highway safety. The proposal is therefore contrary to Chapter 9 of the NPPF (2024), Policies T4, T5 and T6 of the London Plan (2021), and Policy DMD 45 of the Enfield Development Management Document (2014)."
  ],
